# What Christians Search For in the Bible: A Data-Driven Master Test Set for a Scripture Search Engine

## TL;DR

- **Christian Bible search is overwhelmingly “felt-need-first”:** the top real-world queries cluster around emotional survival (anxiety/fear/peace), comfort/God’s presence, hope for the future, strength, and love — YouVersion’s official 2025 report states, “the most searched terms included love, anxiety, and peace,” and **Isaiah 41:10, Jeremiah 29:11, Philippians 4:6, Psalm 23:4, John 3:16, and Romans 8:28** are the recurring head-term verses across every platform.
- **Three query formats coexist and should all be tested:** single keywords (“healing,” “forgiveness”), topic phrases (“bible verses about anxiety,” “scriptures on marriage”), and natural-language questions (“what does the Bible say about worry,” “does God forgive me,” “why is God allowing this”) — with question-format and long-tail “what does the Bible say about X” queries being the fastest-growing format and the specialty of OpenBible.info and GotQuestions.org.
- **This report delivers 700+ categorized test queries** grouped by felt-need, flagged as hard-data-backed vs. reasonably inferred, plus documented seasonal/crisis spikes (fear during COVID, “justice” 100x after George Floyd, John 3:16 at Easter, Jeremiah 29:11 at graduation) and KJV-vs-modern term variants (“charity”/“love”) to build a realistic test suite.

## Key Findings

**1. The felt-need drives the search, not the citation.** Across YouVersion (1B+ installs), Bible Gateway (viewed “hundreds of billions of times” per year), BibleStudyTools, Logos, and OpenBible.info, the same emotional themes recur year after year: fear/anxiety, comfort/God’s presence, hope, strength, peace, healing, and love. YouVersion’s most-engaged verse was **Isaiah 41:10 (“do not fear, for I am with you”)** — named 2025 Verse of the Year, “the fourth time in six years the verse has claimed the top spot” (also #1 in 2018, 2020, 2022, and 2023). **Philippians 4:6 (“do not be anxious about anything”)** won 2024.  The most-searched in-app terms were **“fear” (2020), “prayer” and “peace” (2024), and “love,” “anxiety,” and “peace” (2025).**

**2. Verse-reference head terms are dominated by a small canon.** Per a World Vision UK digital survey (2022) using Ahrefs Keyword Explorer across 172 countries: **John 3:16 had “by far the most monthly searches across the globe at 2.1 million, followed by Jeremiah 29:11 and Philippians 4:13 in joint second at 82,000. John 10:10 was third place with 73,000,”** with Proverbs 3:5 (~58,000), Matthew 28:19 (~50,000), Philippians 4:8 (~42,000), Philippians 4:6 (~37,000), and Romans 8:28 and James 1:3 (~33,000) rounding out the top tier. On Bible Gateway’s own 2024–2025 rankings, however, the **Psalms dominate** — Psalm 23:4 was the #1 read verse  in both 2024 and 2025, with Psalms 23, 91, and 121 filling most of the top 25. 

**3. Platform differences reveal audience differences.** YouVersion (lay/devotional) skews to emotional-promise verses (Isaiah 41:10, Philippians 4:6). Bible Gateway skews to the Psalms (comfort/protection). Logos (pastors/scholars) had all top-25 clicked verses from the New Testament, led by Philippians 4:6 and Matthew 11:28.  GotQuestions.org (which states it averages ~18 million monthly visitors, doctrinal/apologetic) is dominated by theology and ethics questions — its top-5 FAQs are **women pastors, homosexuality, tattoos, eternal security (“once saved always saved”), and masturbation.** A single search engine must serve all these intents.

**4. Search is seasonal and crisis-reactive.** Documented spikes: “fear” became YouVersion’s top term in early 2020 (YouVersion searches rose 80% in 2020, to nearly 600 million). Per Christianity Today citing Bible Gateway, sickness verses “got around 90 times more queries than average” at the March 2020 lockdown; racism/justice/oppression queries spiked to **100 times the average in the week following George Floyd’s death**, and government-authority verses rose **at least 50 times the average on Election Day.** John 3:16, “resurrection,” “risen,” and “crucifixion” spike every Easter; Jeremiah 29:11 and Philippians 4:13 spike at graduation; New Year drives Bible-plan and “fresh start” searches (3+ million subscribed to one-year plans on New Year’s Day 2025).

**5. The gap between “what people feel” and “what Scripture offers.”** Scholars (e.g., Biola’s Carmen Joy Imes) note readers gravitate to “a handful of inspirational or comforting verses without realizing the wide spectrum of spiritual riches available to us in the Psalms.” This matters for search design: a good engine should surface the expected comfort verse AND relevant lesser-known passages.

