## Recommendations

**Stage 1 — Build the core test set now (head terms).** Seed your test suite with the 26 categories above, prioritizing Categories 1–16 (the documented high-volume felt-needs). Include the top verse-reference queries with their known volume tiers: John 3:16 (~2.1M/mo, tier 1); Jeremiah 29:11 / Philippians 4:13 (~82K, tier 2); John 10:10, Proverbs 3:5, Matthew 28:19, Philippians 4:8/4:6, Romans 8:28, James 1:3 (tier 3).  *Benchmark:* the engine should return the community-consensus top verse for each felt-need (e.g., Philippians 4:6 for anxiety, Psalm 23 for grief/comfort) in the top 3 results.

**Stage 2 — Expand to long-tail and questions.** Add the natural-language question variants and long-tail phrases in each category. Pull the actual OpenBible.info topic list (its downloadable `topic-votes.txt` / `topic-scores.zip` files, updated weekly, linked from openbible.info/topics/) and GotQuestions’ published top-20 lists to generate hundreds more real queries verbatim. *Benchmark:* ≥90% of “what does the Bible say about X” queries should resolve to a relevant curated verse set, not a keyword dump.

**Stage 3 — Add seasonal, crisis, and translation-variant test cases.** Build a rotating seasonal test bank (Easter, Christmas, New Year, graduation) and a crisis-simulation bank (health scare, financial hardship, national tragedy). Add KJV/modern term-pair tests. *Benchmark:* “charity” and “love,” “fear not” and “do not be afraid,” “worry” and “anxiety” should return equivalent top results.

**Thresholds that would change the plan:** If your own query logs show a higher share of exact-reference lookups than topical searches (inverting the public pattern), shift investment toward citation parsing and cross-references. If question-format queries exceed ~40% of volume, prioritize a conversational/RAG answer layer over ranked verse lists. If a specific felt-need (e.g., addiction, identity) over-indexes in your logs versus this public data, deepen curation there first.

## Caveats

- **“Most engaged” ≠ “most searched.”** YouVersion’s Verse of the Year reflects highlights/bookmarks/shares,  not raw search queries; Bible Gateway’s ranking reflects page views/downloads; Google volumes reflect search. These are different signals and explain why Isaiah 41:10 tops YouVersion while Psalm 23:4 tops Bible Gateway (Isaiah 41:10 ranked only 26th on Bible Gateway in 2025). Treat cross-platform verse rankings as directional, not identical.
- **Search-volume figures vary by source and method.** The ~2.1M/mo for John 3:16 and ~82K for Jeremiah 29:11/Philippians 4:13 come from the World Vision UK / Ahrefs study across 172 countries; a separate narrower keyword definition yields much lower absolute figures (John 3:16 at ~6,500/mo). Use volume tiers, not precise absolute numbers.
- **Some queries are [INFERRED].** Categories like Identity (17) and Depression/Loneliness (7) are extrapolated from documented adjacent data and topical-index structures rather than a named ranking; they are realistic but should be validated against your own logs.
- **The precise OpenBible.info topic ranking was not directly retrievable.** Its authoritative ranked list lives in downloadable data files; the perennial top topics reported here (love, faith, marriage, forgiveness, fear, healing, hope, prayer) are synthesized from OpenBible’s own 2016 election chart, its “verses on…” analysis, and third-party reviews/SEO tools. Download and sort `topic-votes.txt` for exact current rankings.
- **US/English-centric.** Most granular data is US/English. YouVersion documents country differences (e.g., Philippians 4 was 2024’s top verse across 17 countries;  Joshua 1:9 ranks higher in Australia;  Central/South America favors Genesis verses),  so a global engine should localize.
- **Recency:** Data spans 2020–2026 annual reports; the strongest recent points are YouVersion 2024/2025, Bible Gateway 2024/2025, BibleStudyTools 2024, and State of the Bible 2025/2026. Verse popularity is stable year-to-year, so the list should age well, but re-pull annual reports each December.